chapter_notes.1B:
This is a limited-exposure Chapter 1B case: Mexico had no nuclear arsenal, AMLO’s 2022 role was mostly treaty/diplomatic stewardship and civilian nuclear oversight, and I found no 2022 ruler-attributed nuclear-use, brinkmanship, proliferation, or command-and-control choice to score. Reused evidence: none.

**New Evidence**
- [Constitución Política de los Estados Unidos Mexicanos](https://www.ordenjuridico.gob.mx/Documentos/Federal/html/wo14166.html) — executive power is vested in a single president.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Constitución Política de los Estados Unidos Mexicanos","publisher":"Orden Jurídico Nacional","publication_date":"1917-02-05 (current text updated on site)","url":"https://www.ordenjuridico.gob.mx/Documentos/Federal/html/wo14166.html","claim":"Article 80 vests the executive power of the federation in a single individual called the President of the United Mexican States.","locator":"Article 80 snippet","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official constitutional text; stable and authoritative for office structure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly applicable to 2022 as the governing constitutional framework.","ruler_attribution":"Structural authority held by AMLO as president; this does not itself show any nuclear-specific personal choice.","contrary_evidence":["Mexico is non-nuclear, so this office does not imply command over a nuclear arsenal.","Formal executive authority does not prove operational control over civilian nuclear safety details."],"lenses":["1B.10"]}

- [Constitución Política de los Estados Unidos Mexicanos](https://www.ordenjuridico.gob.mx/Documentos/Federal/html/wo14166.html) — foreign policy and treaty authority are executive, but Senate approval and peaceful principles constrain it.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Constitución Política de los Estados Unidos Mexicanos","publisher":"Orden Jurídico Nacional","publication_date":"1917-02-05 (current text updated on site)","url":"https://www.ordenjuridico.gob.mx/Documentos/Federal/html/wo14166.html","claim":"Article 89, fraction X, gives the president authority to direct foreign policy and conclude treaties, subject to Senate approval, and requires peaceful settlement and non-use-of-force principles.","locator":"Article 89, fraction X snippet","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official constitutional text; directly states the executive's foreign-policy power and constraints.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly applicable to 2022.","ruler_attribution":"AMLO had treaty-diplomacy authority in 2022, but the legal text is institutional rather than personal nuclear-risk evidence.","contrary_evidence":["The Senate remains a real constraint.","These powers concern diplomacy, not weapons custody or nuclear command."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.8","1B.10"]}

- [Versión estenográfica. 4° Informe de Gobierno](https://www.gob.mx/amlo/articulos/version-estenografica-4-informe-de-gobierno) — AMLO personally presented his 2022 government report as president constitutional and commander in chief.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Versión estenográfica. 4° Informe de Gobierno","publisher":"Presidencia de la República / gob.mx","publication_date":"2022-09-01","url":"https://www.gob.mx/amlo/articulos/version-estenografica-4-informe-de-gobierno","claim":"The official transcript identifies Andrés Manuel López Obrador as the constitutional president of Mexico and commander in chief when he presented the fourth government report from Palacio Nacional.","locator":"Opening transcript lines","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official transcript dated the day of the event.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 match.","ruler_attribution":"Direct AMLO attribution, but the content is a self-report rather than independent verification.","contrary_evidence":["The report is inherently self-justifying.","It does not show any nuclear-risk decision or safety reform by itself."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.10"]}

- [Country List: Mexico](https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=MX) — Mexico’s nuclear activities are covered by safeguards under Tlatelolco and the NPT, plus an additional protocol.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Country List : Office of Legal Affairs : IAEA","publisher":"International Atomic Energy Agency","publication_date":"current registry; last updated 2026-02-24 / 2026-03-23","url":"https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=MX","claim":"Mexico has safeguards agreements in force covering the Treaty of Tlatelolco, the NPT, and an additional protocol to the safeguards arrangement.","locator":"Safeguards Agreements section, entries 604, 1038, and 1877","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative IAEA legal registry for country safeguards status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Strong inherited-baseline fit for 2022; the registry is current but the agreements were already in force.","ruler_attribution":"State-level safeguard architecture inherited by AMLO; not a personal nuclear-command act.","contrary_evidence":["This shows peaceful-use monitoring, not proof of perfect safety.","It is institutional context, not evidence of a 2022 AMLO decision."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.9","1B.10"]}

- [PRIS - Country Details: Mexico](https://pris.iaea.org/pris/CountryStatistics/CountryDetails.aspx?current=MX) — Mexico had two operating civilian reactors at Laguna Verde.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PRIS - Country Details","publisher":"International Atomic Energy Agency","publication_date":"current registry; last update 2026-07-21","url":"https://pris.iaea.org/pris/CountryStatistics/CountryDetails.aspx?current=MX","claim":"Mexico’s operating nuclear fleet consists of two civilian reactors at Laguna Verde, both in operational status.","locator":"Reactors table, lines 53-57","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IAEA PRIS is the standard authoritative reactor-status database.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Moderate fit: current registry, but the civilian reactor configuration is stable and consistent with the 2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"Civilian nuclear infrastructure under Mexican state oversight; not evidence of weapons authority or nuclear escalation.","contrary_evidence":["A civilian nuclear sector still creates safety and regulatory responsibility.","The page does not show AMLO personally changing reactor safety policy in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.9","1B.10"]}

- [UN Treaty Collection: NPT](https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=0800000280120b8d) — Mexico ratified the NPT in 1970.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNTC action details: Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"1970-05-25","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=0800000280120b8d","claim":"Mexico is recorded by the UN depositary as having ratified the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons.","locator":"Treaty Registration Number 10485 record","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN treaty depositary record is authoritative for ratification status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited-baseline evidence; long before 2022 but still binding in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"State commitment predates AMLO and is inherited policy context, not AMLO-specific conduct.","contrary_evidence":["This is a treaty commitment, not proof of active 2022 risk-reduction action.","It does not by itself establish personal responsibility under 1B."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

- [UN Treaty Collection: TPNW](https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=08000002804e4e95) — Mexico ratified the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons in 2021.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNTC action details: Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"2021-01-22","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=08000002804e4e95","claim":"Mexico is recorded by the UN depositary as having ratified the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons.","locator":"Treaty Registration Number 56487 record","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN treaty depositary record is authoritative for ratification status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Strong inherited-baseline fit for 2022, though the ratification preceded AMLO’s 2022 actions.","ruler_attribution":"State-level commitment inherited by AMLO; not evidence of a 2022 personal nuclear-risk decision.","contrary_evidence":["This is normative commitment, not operational proof of de-escalation choices in 2022.","It does not show AMLO personally advanced the treaty in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

- [CTBTO Status of Signatures and Ratifications](https://www.ctbto.org/our-mission/states-signatories) — Mexico ratified the CTBT in 1999.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Status of Signature and Ratification","publisher":"CTBTO","publication_date":"current registry; accessed 2026-07-22","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/our-mission/states-signatories","claim":"The CTBTO status table records Mexico as having signed the CTBT on 24 September 1996 and ratified it on 5 October 1999.","locator":"Mexico row in status table","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CTBTO treaty-status table is authoritative for signature and ratification dates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited-baseline evidence; strong context for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This is inherited disarmament posture, not AMLO-specific conduct.","contrary_evidence":["The CTBT is about testing restraint, not a full measure of existential-risk governance.","No 2022 AMLO-specific testing decision appears here."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

- [Conclusion of the first meeting of the Nuclear Weapons Ban Treaty in Vienna](https://www.gob.mx/sre/prensa/conclusion-of-the-first-meeting-of-the-nuclear-weapons-ban-treaty-in-vienna) — Mexico publicly reaffirmed its disarmament leadership in June 2022.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Conclusion of the first meeting of the Nuclear Weapons Ban Treaty in Vienna","publisher":"Secretaría de Relaciones Exteriores","publication_date":"2022-06-24","url":"https://www.gob.mx/sre/prensa/conclusion-of-the-first-meeting-of-the-nuclear-weapons-ban-treaty-in-vienna","claim":"Mexico welcomed the first meeting of the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons states parties and said it was one of the treaty's architects, reaffirming its leadership in nuclear disarmament.","locator":"Headline, opening paragraph, and first bullet points","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary foreign-ministry statement, contemporaneous and directly relevant to 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Government of Mexico under AMLO, but this is institutional foreign-policy signaling rather than a unique personal nuclear-risk choice.","contrary_evidence":["The source is celebratory and self-presenting.","It shows stance and diplomacy, not technical command or safety reform."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.10"]}

- [Conclusion of the 8th Summer School on Nuclear Disarmament and Nonproliferation at the Foreign Ministry](https://www.gob.mx/sre/prensa/conclusion-of-the-8th-summer-school-on-nuclear-disarmament-and-nonproliferation-at-the-foreign-ministry) — Mexico invested in disarmament expertise and training in 2022.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Conclusion of the 8th Summer School on Nuclear Disarmament and Nonproliferation at the Foreign Ministry","publisher":"Secretaría de Relaciones Exteriores","publication_date":"2022-07-18","url":"https://www.gob.mx/sre/prensa/conclusion-of-the-8th-summer-school-on-nuclear-disarmament-and-nonproliferation-at-the-foreign-ministry","claim":"The Foreign Ministry hosted the eighth annual Summer School on Nuclear Disarmament and Nonproliferation for diplomats from Latin America and the Caribbean, with 23 officials from 22 countries participating.","locator":"Opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary institutional release with a clear date and program description.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional capacity-building under AMLO’s administration; not a direct personal nuclear-command act.","contrary_evidence":["Training and convening are supportive but indirect.","This does not prove any specific 2022 risk-bearing decision by AMLO himself."],"lenses":["1B.7"]}

- [Mexico: Historic promoter of nuclear disarmament and nonproliferation](https://www.gob.mx/sre/en/articulos/mexico-historic-promoter-of-nuclear-disarmament-and-nonproliferation) — Tlatelolco created a nuclear-weapon-free zone and the region forbids possession and testing of nuclear weapons.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Historic promoter of nuclear disarmament and nonproliferation","publisher":"Secretaría de Relaciones Exteriores","publication_date":"2016-02-09","url":"https://www.gob.mx/sre/en/articulos/mexico-historic-promoter-of-nuclear-disarmament-and-nonproliferation","claim":"The Foreign Ministry says the Treaty of Tlatelolco created a nuclear-weapon-free zone in Latin America and the Caribbean and that the treaty’s goals include ensuring the absence of nuclear weapons and limiting nuclear material to peaceful purposes.","locator":"Article text under 'What are the goals of the Treaty of Tlatelolco?'","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary historical ministry statement; not contested on basic treaty facts.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Historical baseline rather than 2022-specific, but highly relevant to inherited posture.","ruler_attribution":"Inherited state tradition, not AMLO-specific conduct.","contrary_evidence":["This is a historical institutional posture, not a 2022 action.","It explains the baseline but does not show what AMLO newly did in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.6","1B.10"]}

- [La deslegitimación de las armas, el desarme y la protección de la seguridad humana](https://re.sre.gob.mx/rmpe/index.php/rmpe/article/view/519) — scholarly Mexican foreign-policy analysis framing disarmament as longstanding but detached from day-to-day security concerns.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"La deslegitimación de las armas, el desarme y la protección de la seguridad humana","publisher":"Revista Mexicana de Política Exterior","publication_date":"2022-03-16","url":"https://re.sre.gob.mx/rmpe/index.php/rmpe/article/view/519","claim":"The article’s abstract says disarmament has been on Mexico’s foreign-policy agenda for 60 years and asks whether nuclear disarmament is practicable in light of 21st-century security realities.","locator":"Abstract","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Published analytical piece from a Mexican foreign-policy journal; useful context, but it is interpretive rather than a primary record.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact 2022 publication date, though the analysis is general rather than AMLO-specific.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual to Mexico’s policy tradition; not direct evidence of AMLO’s own decision-making.","contrary_evidence":["Interpretive scholarship can overgeneralize from tradition.","It does not identify a specific 2022 nuclear-risk choice by AMLO."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I checked a broad set of documents across constitutional law, UN treaty records, IAEA safeguards/PRIS, CTBTO status tables, SRE/OPANAL releases, and a Mexican policy journal. Most additional hits were duplicates of the same treaty facts, pre-2022 history, or blocked/JS-gated pages. I rejected or deprioritized several items because they were out-of-period for the ruler-period question, restated the same legal status in a different wrapper, or were too general to show a 2022 AMLO choice. The main non-duplicate evidence families retained were:
- office/authority constraints,
- Mexico’s non-nuclear treaty posture,
- civilian nuclear infrastructure under safeguards,
- 2022 disarmament diplomacy,
- 2022 institution-building/training.

**Remaining Gaps**
- No source found showing a 2022 AMLO-specific nuclear command, accident-prevention, or escalation decision.
- No evidence found of any 2022 ruler-attributed choice in cyber, biological, or AI catastrophic-risk governance that clearly belongs in 1B rather than other chapters.
- The civilian nuclear safety record could be checked more deeply, but that would likely add context rather than change the chapter’s basic exposure classification.
- If you want a fuller 1B package, the only materially useful next search would be around Mexican civilian nuclear safety/regulatory changes in 2022, not weapons policy.

