**Search and Rejection Summary**
I considered roughly 30 distinct documents across the core evidence families:
- 4 UN Security Council meeting records on Ukraine from January, February, March, and September 2022.
- 4 UN General Assembly / UN Human Rights Council records on Ukraine from March, April, and November 2022.
- 6 gob.mx / SRE releases and AMLO transcript pages on Ukraine, evacuations, sanctions, and the peace proposal.
- 2 Reuters-origin reports via Euronews on the peace plan and Lavrov meeting.
- 2 local-language media pieces on the France-Mexico humanitarian draft.
- Several later 2024 retrospectives that referenced the 2022 initiative but were not necessary once the primary 2022 record was in hand.

I rejected or downweighted documents that were:
- duplicates in English/Spanish of the same government release,
- later retrospectives that merely restated 2022 events,
- out-of-scope for Chapter 2B because they were about domestic policy rather than external conduct,
- or too general to add anything beyond the local prior and the official UN record.

The main weak/blocked items were some gob.mx pages and UN digital-library pages that were hard to open directly, but their search snippets were still usable for narrow sponsor-list or headline-level claims. I did not rely on any page where I could not identify a distinct material claim.

**Remaining Gaps**
- There is still no evidence that Mexico had any military intervention, proxy war, or cross-border coercive operation in 2022. That is a real exposure gap, not a missing fact to paper over.
- I did not find evidence that AMLO's peace committee produced an actual ceasefire, negotiation track, or formal settlement in 2022.
- I did not find a meaningful contrary record of Mexican aggression, prolongation of war, or unlawful civilian harm. That is consistent with the limited-exposure profile, but it also means the chapter is judged mostly on diplomacy and restraint rather than battlefield conduct.
- If you want more depth, the only search area likely to add material value is follow-through on the September 2022 peace initiative, especially any documented UN, Russian, Ukrainian, or third-party reaction after 2022.

chapter_notes.3B:
**Reused evidence**
None beyond the local prior and ruler identity.

**New Evidence**
- HRW’s 2023 Mexico chapter is the best single broad summary of the 2022 domestic-safety environment: it says violent crime stayed at historic highs under López Obrador, the military’s domestic role expanded, police functions were transferred to Defense in 2022, over 105,000 people were missing by September, and the journalist/defender protection mechanism was under-resourced.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Mexico","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/mexico","claim":"HRW says violent crime remained at historic highs under López Obrador, the military's domestic role expanded and police functions were formally transferred to the Ministry of Defense in 2022, over 105,000 people were officially missing by September, and the journalist/defender protection mechanism lacked resources.","locator":"Opening keynote plus Violence and Impunity / Military Abuses / Disappearances / Attacks on Journalists and Human Rights Defenders sections","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major rights-monitoring summary with multiple independently sourced claims and direct 2022 coverage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 events summarized in a 2023 review.","ruler_attribution":"National policy and institutional environment under AMLO; the abuses are mostly carried out by military, police, prosecutors, or criminal groups rather than by a single personal order.","contrary_evidence":["HRW also notes the National Search Commission and protection mechanism existed and took steps to improve search/identification.","The report describes formal systems, but says they were insufficient."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.10"]}

- Amnesty’s 2022/23 Mexico report is a strong independent counterweight: it says the government kept stigmatizing feminists and defenders, security forces used violence against women protesters, journalist killings stayed at record levels, more than 109,000 people were missing by year-end, domestic security militarization was locked in through 2028, and the National Guard used excessive force.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexiko 2022 | Amnesty International Report 2022/23","publisher":"Amnesty International Deutschland","publication_date":"2023-03-28","url":"https://www.amnesty.de/informieren/amnesty-report/mexiko-2022","claim":"Amnesty says the government continued stigmatizing feminists and human rights defenders, security forces used violence against women protesters, journalist killings remained at record levels, more than 109,000 people were missing at year-end, militarization of domestic security was extended through 2028, and the National Guard used excessive force in several operations.","locator":"Background / freedom of expression / excessive force / disappearances sections","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual rights report with clear 2022 coverage and concrete event-level allegations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 reporting window.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly state and security-force conduct under AMLO; some incidents are state-level or operational rather than direct presidential orders.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes some remedies and judicial steps in individual cases, including Ayotzinapa.","It is a rights-monitoring synthesis, not a court finding for every incident."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.3","3B.5","3B.7","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

- ARTICLE 19’s 2022 annual report is the clearest quantitative source on press repression and state-linked stigma.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Voices Against Indifference","publisher":"ARTICLE 19","publication_date":"2023-03-28","url":"https://www.article19.org/resources/mexico-voices-against-indifference/","claim":"ARTICLE 19 reports 696 attacks on the press in 2022, one every 13 hours; 42.53% were committed directly by state actors; the executive branch made stigmatising comments at least 176 times; and 10 media companies received 54.8% of government advertising spend.","locator":"Report summary and statistics sections","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed annual press-freedom dataset from a specialized monitor, with clear 2022 statistics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly relevant to AMLO-era public discourse and federal advertising control, though not every attack is directly ordered by him.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes conditions that predate AMLO, so it should not be over-read as personal command over every abuse.","Advertising concentration is a structural influence mechanism, not a complete explanation of violence."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.3","3B.5","3B.6","3B.8","3B.10"]}

- ARTICLE 19’s January 2022 report captures the start-of-year journalist-killing spike.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: State must take immediate action after murder of four journalists","publisher":"ARTICLE 19","publication_date":"2022-01-31","url":"https://www.article19.org/resources/mexico-state-must-take-immediate-action-after-murder-of-three-journalists/","claim":"ARTICLE 19 says four journalists were killed in January 2022 and that Mexico had already recorded 28 journalist murders during López Obrador’s presidency, calling for urgent independent investigations.","locator":"Opening paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights-monitoring report with exact 2022 incident count.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"The killings themselves are not all federally attributed, but the failure to stop and investigate them is relevant to AMLO-era domestic protection.","contrary_evidence":["The report emphasizes investigation failures more than direct federal ordering of the killings.","It is strongest on impunity and protection failure, not on motive attribution for each murder."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.9","3B.10"]}

- RSF’s January 2022 report is useful for early-year press insecurity and the call to strengthen protection.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 begins with bloodbath for journalists in Mexico","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2022-01-21","url":"https://rsf.org/en/2022-begins-bloodbath-journalists-mexico-0","claim":"RSF says Mexico started 2022 with the killings of two journalists in one week, described the situation as a bloodbath for the Mexican media, and urged the authorities to reinforce journalist-protection mechanisms.","locator":"Lead paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous specialized monitoring from a major press-freedom organization.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Federal protection failures matter more than personal order attribution here; the article is about the national safety environment under AMLO.","contrary_evidence":["The piece does not prove AMLO personally directed the murders.","It is a crisis snapshot rather than a full institutional audit."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.10"]}

- CPJ’s 2022 briefing provides another independent count of the journalist-killing crisis and its impunity context.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"In 2022, journalist killings continue unabated in Mexico amid a climate of impunity","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-08","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/08/in-2022-journalist-killings-continue-unabated-in-mexico-amid-a-climate-of-impunity/amp/","claim":"CPJ says at least 13 journalists had been killed in Mexico by late August 2022, the highest number it had documented in a single year, and described a climate of impunity with authorities often slow and inadequate.","locator":"Opening section and opening paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialized monitor; the page is a briefing rather than a formal audit, but the numbers are specific and contemporaneous.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Applies to the AMLO-era domestic protection environment; the article does not attribute each death to the federal executive.","contrary_evidence":["CPJ notes some cases may be cartel-related or hard to motive-match.","It does not separate federal from state or criminal responsibility in every case."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.9","3B.10"]}

- Amnesty’s protest reporting gives concrete evidence of police abuse against peaceful women students and the wider protest climate.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The right to protest is under threat in Mexico","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-05-18","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/05/right-to-protest-under-threat-mexico/","claim":"Amnesty says police violently repressed a peaceful 2021 student protest, used racist, classist and misogynistic insults, threatened sexual violence, and left the abuses unpunished a year later; it also says this reflected a wider pattern of repression against women protesters in Mexico.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and Mactumactzá incident description","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed eyewitness-based rights report with explicit protest-treatment allegations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The incident predates 2022 but the article is a 2022 assessment of the continuing pattern.","ruler_attribution":"The abuse is mostly state-level, but it informs the national safety climate under AMLO and the federal response to protest repression.","contrary_evidence":["The piece is centered on state police, not direct federal command.","It is strongest on repression patterns, not on a single federal order."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.3","3B.7","3B.8","3B.9"]}

- AMLO’s own March 8 briefing is a useful positive counterpoint on protest rights and non-repression claims.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Versión estenográfica de la conferencia de prensa matutina del presidente Andrés Manuel López Obrador","publisher":"Presidencia de la República","publication_date":"2022-03-08","url":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/08-03-22-version-estenografica-de-la-conferencia-de-prensa-matutina-del-presidente-andres-manuel-lopez-obrador/","claim":"AMLO said there was no repression, that nobody was prevented from demonstrating, and that the government had an obligation to guarantee liberties and the right to dissent; he also said women and other groups could protest.","locator":"Lines 106-124 of the transcript","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential transcript with direct quotes from AMLO on protest rights.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Direct AMLO attribution.","contrary_evidence":["This is rhetoric and self-description, not proof that subordinate police or military behaved accordingly everywhere.","The same transcript also contains language about provocations and infiltration."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}

- The same March 8 briefing also contains a concrete accountability claim after a Guard-related fatality.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Versión estenográfica de la conferencia de prensa matutina del presidente Andrés Manuel López Obrador","publisher":"Presidencia de la República","publication_date":"2022-03-08","url":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/08-03-22-version-estenografica-de-la-conferencia-de-prensa-matutina-del-presidente-andres-manuel-lopez-obrador/","claim":"After a CNDH recommendation concerning the death of Jessica Estrella Solís in a Guardia Nacional-related incident, AMLO said the cabinet had agreed to comply with the recommendation's five points.","locator":"Lines 463-466 of the transcript","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary transcript with a specific remedy commitment tied to a rights-body recommendation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Direct AMLO attribution on federal compliance with a rights-body recommendation.","contrary_evidence":["The underlying incident shows lethal force by the National Guard, so the positive remedy does not erase the abuse.","The source is a self-report of compliance, not external proof of completion."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.9"]}

- AMLO’s November 2022 remarks are the clearest direct incitement-style evidence in the packet, even if framed as political polemic.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Versión estenográfica de la conferencia de prensa matutina del presidente Andrés Manuel López Obrador","publisher":"Presidencia de la República","publication_date":"2022-11-16","url":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/16-11-22-version-estenografica-de-la-conferencia-de-prensa-matutina-del-presidente-andres-manuel-lopez-obrador/","claim":"AMLO said conservatives are racist, classist, discriminatory, corrupt, and very partisan of violence, while describing them as a political force opposed to his project.","locator":"Lines 388-389 of the transcript","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential transcript with direct, dated language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Direct AMLO attribution.","contrary_evidence":["This was political polemic rather than a direct call for violence.","It targets opponents broadly, so it is more relevant to stigmatization than to a specific abuse order."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.6","3B.8"]}

- The 9 September 2022 legal reform formally deepened militarization of public security.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DECRETO por el que se reforman, adicionan y derogan diversas disposiciones... en Materia de Guardia Nacional y Seguridad Pública","publisher":"Orden Jurídico Nacional / Diario Oficial de la Federación","publication_date":"2022-09-09","url":"https://www.ordenjuridico.gob.mx/Documentos/Federal/html/wo13238.html","claim":"The decree published in the Diario Oficial formalized reforms placing the National Guard's operational and administrative control under Sedena and changing related military and security laws.","locator":"DOF publication notice and reform articles","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official legal text and publication record for the 2022 reform.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects a presidentially driven federal policy choice under AMLO.","contrary_evidence":["Supporters framed the move as administrative discipline and public safety management.","The decree does not itself prove any specific abuse, but it increases the risk of abuse and impunity."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.5","3B.10"]}

- The federal protection mechanism had real capacity and did expand by early 2022, which is the main favorable institutional evidence.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Protección a personas periodistas y defensoras de derechos humanos es obligación de Estado: Segob","publisher":"Secretaría de Gobernación","publication_date":"2022-01-26","url":"https://www.gob.mx/segob/prensa/proteccion-a-personas-periodistas-y-defensoras-de-derechos-humanos-es-obligacion-de-estado-segob","claim":"The Segob protection mechanism said it had grown from 758 people at the start of the administration to 1,508 beneficiaries by January 2022, including 495 journalists and 1,023 human rights defenders.","locator":"Opening paragraphs of the comunicado","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government statement with concrete beneficiary counts and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional federal protection capacity under AMLO; the data do not prove the mechanism was sufficient everywhere.","contrary_evidence":["The same mechanism was still described by independent monitors as under-resourced and unable to meet all protection needs.","Growing beneficiary counts do not automatically mean successful protection."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.6","3B.7"]}

- Reuters’ Pegasus story is the best concise evidence that surveillance against defenders did not stop under AMLO.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pegasus spyware attacks in Mexico continued under Lopez Obrador -report","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-10-02","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/pegasus-spyware-attacks-in-mexico-continued-under-lopez-obrador-report-2903551","claim":"Reuters reported that at least three human rights investigators in Mexico were infected with Pegasus during López Obrador’s term despite government promises to stop using the spyware.","locator":"Opening Reuters lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is a strong wire source; the specific claim is directly dated and attributable.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"This is relevant to federal conduct under AMLO even if the operator of each infection is not named in the lead.","contrary_evidence":["The article is based on a report, not a full forensic adjudication in the story itself.","It does not prove AMLO personally ordered every infection."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.5","3B.8"]}

- The UN Committee on Enforced Disappearances’ 2022 visit report is the strongest multilateral critique of Mexico’s disappearance response.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Report of the Committee on Enforced Disappearances on its visit to Mexico under article 33 of the Convention","publisher":"United Nations Committee on Enforced Disappearances","publication_date":"2022-05-16","url":"https://docstore.ohchr.org/SelfServices/FilesHandler.ashx?enc=xfPmnugUdhHJq4VZ3vrE4eZfqQRaRWKov3yTCeeGOPYg0uyArMBWHcSZ6aeqhJ9hkcaKcFP0GjUeT8vc8COLIQ%3D%3D","claim":"The Committee said Mexico should strengthen institutions, searches and investigations, remove obstacles to taking disappearance cases to court, address the forensic crisis, facilitate truth/justice/reparation, and protect victims and searchers.","locator":"Observations and recommendations, section I and related subsections","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative UN monitoring report with direct policy recommendations after a country visit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Applies to the AMLO administration’s handling of disappearances and investigations, though many failures sit with prosecutors, forensic institutions, and subnational authorities.","contrary_evidence":["The report does not say the federal government made no progress at all.","It is a recommendations document, not a court judgment."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.4","3B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across rights monitors, UN bodies, official Mexican sources, and wire/local reporting. I kept only items that added something materially distinct to the 2022 domestic-safety record. I rejected or downweighted:
- duplicate journalist-killing stories that repeated the same deaths without adding new attribution or remedy information,
- older 2020-2021 protest reports except where they were used only as background for a 2022 assessment,
- later 2023/2024 follow-ups when the 2022 summary already captured the relevant facts,
- general crime or macro-governance coverage that lacked a physical-safety nexus,
- state-level incidents that were too decoupled from federal authority to help with AMLO attribution,
- and several blocked gob.mx or UN document pages when the search snippet did not add a distinct material claim.

**Remaining gaps**
- The evidence is strong on recurring repression, violence against journalists, militarization, and weak remedies, but weaker on a single clean federal command chain for each abuse.
- I did not find a source that proves AMLO personally ordered a specific arrest, beating, or killing in 2022; most of the record is institutional, rhetorical, or policy-level.
- The best positive evidence is real but limited: the protection mechanism expanded, and AMLO sometimes endorsed protest rights and compliance with a human-rights recommendation. That does not neutralize the broader pattern.
- I did not find enough to say federal protection reliably improved on the ground for women protesters, journalists, indigenous defenders, or search groups in 2022.

chapter_notes.4B:
Reused evidence: none. The prior constitutional office item was not materially specific to 4B electoral freedom, so I did not count it as reused chapter evidence.

New evidence:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Más de 90% de votantes quieren que el presidente de la República continúe su mandato","publisher":"Presidencia de la República 2018-2024 | Gobierno | gob.mx","publication_date":"2022-04-10","url":"https://www.gob.mx/amlo/prensa/mas-de-90-de-votantes-quieren-que-el-presidente-de-la-republica-continue-su-mandato","claim":"After the recall vote, AMLO said more than 90% supported his continuing mandate, described the exercise as a step for democracy, and reiterated that he would not govern beyond September 2024.","locator":"official press statement / closing paragraphs of the release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government statement by the ruler himself, directly attributable on dates and intent, though self-serving on effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period evidence on contestability and succession signaling.","ruler_attribution":"Direct AMLO speech as president; fully attributable as his own public position, though not proof of implementation.","contrary_evidence":["A recall vote is not the same as a free competitive election, and the statement does not by itself prove broad respect for independent institutions."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Este video en el que AMLO habla de ampliar su mandato fue editado; él rechaza esa posibilidad","publisher":"AFP Fact Check","publication_date":"2022-10-25","url":"https://factual.afp.com/doc.afp.com.32M67T7","claim":"AFP found that a circulated video suggesting AMLO proposed extending his mandate was edited; the full remarks show he rejected staying in office beyond September 30, 2024.","locator":"article body and transcript comparison","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent fact-check built around the transcript and the original public remarks; strong for the no-extension claim.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 evidence about term-limit signaling during the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to AMLO's own public statements; useful for intention, not a legal safeguard by itself.","contrary_evidence":["It is evidence of stated restraint, not a completed transfer of power or a tested refusal under real succession pressure."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexican Congress rejects president's electoral reform bid","publisher":"Reuters / Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-12-06","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/mexican-congress-rejects-presidents-electoral-reform-bid-2958679","claim":"Mexico's lower house rejected a president-backed electoral reform that critics said could undermine electoral independence; the bill sought to make the National Electoral Institute smaller and more politically controlled, and AMLO immediately pursued an alternate route requiring only a simple majority.","locator":"lines 419-425","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a concrete legislative episode with named vote counts and direct attribution to the president's reform effort.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 target-period legislative action by the ruler and his coalition.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable because the reform was backed by AMLO and the article describes his immediate follow-on strategy.","contrary_evidence":["AMLO defended the proposal as strengthening democracy and reducing the influence of economic interests in politics.","Congress, not AMLO alone, blocked the constitutional version."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.4","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Tens of thousands protest Mexican president's electoral reform plan","publisher":"Reuters Archive Licensing","publication_date":"2022-11-13","url":"https://reuters.screenocean.com/r/1697262","claim":"Tens of thousands protested AMLO's electoral reform plan in Mexico City, saying they feared it would concentrate power in the hands of the government.","locator":"story summary / Reuters archive licensing record","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters archive summary is clear on the event and public reaction, though it is a compressed secondary record rather than the full article.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 protest against a ruler-sponsored political reform.","ruler_attribution":"The protest was against AMLO's own reform plan, so the attribution to his conduct is direct, though the protest itself is not proof of coercion.","contrary_evidence":["Mass protest also shows active civic resistance and did not itself prove state repression.","The summary alone does not document the government's immediate response."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico","publisher":"Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/digital-news-report/2022/mexico","claim":"The country page says the Mexican news industry contends with the president's daily attacks on its credibility, that AMLO's morning briefings became more pointed with a 'Who's Who' segment targeting journalists and publications, and that journalists continued to be murdered in 2022.","locator":"lines 33-39","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"A reputable annual country analysis from the Reuters Institute synthesizing the 2022 information environment and press climate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 press-environment evidence, though some homicide figures are cumulative/contextual.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to AMLO's daily briefings and public messaging; journalist killings themselves are mostly context and not wholly ruler-caused.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes that murders were often linked to organized crime, so not every press killing can be attributed to the president.","Mexico still had active independent media and visible public criticism."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Versión estenográfica. Conferencia de prensa del presidente Andrés Manuel López Obrador del 7 de septiembre de 2022","publisher":"Presidencia de la República 2018-2024 | Gobierno | gob.mx","publication_date":"2022-09-07","url":"https://www.gob.mx/amlo/articulos/version-estenografica-conferencia-de-prensa-del-presidente-andres-manuel-lopez-obrador-del-7-de-septiembre-de-2022","claim":"The official transcript shows the president and his spokesperson presenting the 'Quién es quién en las mentiras' segment as a weekly summary of lies in the media and social networks, and using it to single out critical outlets.","locator":"search-result excerpt / opening transcript text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary transcript from the presidential website documenting the ruler's own messaging and framing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 ruler speech and media-control messaging.","ruler_attribution":"Fully attributable to AMLO's presidency because it is his official morning conference and his administration's recurring media segment.","contrary_evidence":["The government framed the segment as correcting misinformation and protecting the public's right to information.","The transcript shows rhetoric and framing, not a legal censorship order."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico's president, a fierce press critic, offers benefits to journalists","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-04-07","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/04/07/us-mexico-president-media","claim":"Reuters reported that AMLO said his government would channel a quarter of its publicity budget into health insurance and pensions for poorer journalists, while noting the move could affect traditional media revenues and reflected a fractious relationship with critical outlets.","locator":"lines 660-670","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a concrete public-policy announcement, with useful context about press relations and likely side effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy and rhetoric on the media environment.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to AMLO as a government action; the press-effect analysis is interpretive but tied to his announcement.","contrary_evidence":["This could be read as welfare support for precarious journalists rather than pure coercion.","The article itself notes a possible revenue hit to traditional media, so the measure is mixed rather than clearly protective."],"lenses":["4B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ejecutivo Federal abusa del poder público para estigmatizar a la prensa","publisher":"ARTICLE 19 MX-CA","publication_date":"2022-02-13","url":"https://articulo19.org/ejecutivo-federal-abusa-del-poder-publico-para-estigmatizar-a-la-prensa/","claim":"ARTICLE 19 condemned AMLO's morning conference attack on Carlos Loret de Mola, saying the president denigrated and criminalized the journalist by exposing alleged income data in a way that was intimidating and violated press freedom and privacy standards.","locator":"search-result excerpt / opening article text","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous rights-organization statement describing a specific ruler-attributed press incident.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 incident in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to AMLO because the complaint concerns his own public conference and remarks.","contrary_evidence":["The government could argue it was criticizing alleged false reporting rather than suppressing journalism.","The article is advocacy-oriented, so the precise legal characterization is stronger than the normative conclusion."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Mexico","publisher":"USDOS – U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089118.html","claim":"The report says independent media often self-censored due to fear of reprisals, that politicians including President López Obrador publicly discredited journalists as biased or corrupt, and that the 'Who's Who in Lies' segment worsened official discrediting of press workers.","locator":"lines 139-140 and 237-239, plus executive summary context","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual government human-rights report with direct factual descriptions of the 2022 press environment and state treatment of journalists.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022; strong because it directly reports the target year's conditions.","ruler_attribution":"AMLO-specific in the sense that the report names him as a discrediting actor and ties the media segment to his morning conference; other pressures are broader context.","contrary_evidence":["The report also says the government generally respected free expression and that independent media remained active.","Some intimidation came from criminal groups, not only the president or his administration."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New Pegasus Spyware Abuses Identified in Mexico","publisher":"The Citizen Lab, University of Toronto","publication_date":"2022-10-02","url":"https://citizenlab.ca/research/new-pegasus-spyware-abuses-identified-in-mexico/","claim":"Citizen Lab and R3D found Pegasus infections against Mexican journalists and a human rights defender between 2019 and 2021, after Mexico's current president had assured the public that the government no longer used the spyware and that there would be no further abuses.","locator":"lines 60-62 and 95-100","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Technical forensic work from a respected research lab, with clear victim identification and temporal attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2022 and directly relevant to the ruler's 2022 position on surveillance, though the infections themselves span 2019-2021.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable at least to the current administration's denial and failure to stop the abuse; the report does not by itself name every operator or issue a full command chain.","contrary_evidence":["The infections are partly retrospective to 2019-2021, so they are best used as evidence of continuing abuse under the administration rather than a new 2022 incident.","The report does not by itself prove that AMLO personally ordered each infection."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Army used Pegasus spyware against journalists and activists","publisher":"ARTICLE 19","publication_date":"2022-10-04","url":"https://www.article19.org/resources/mexico-army-spyware-journalists-activists/","claim":"ARTICLE 19 said the Army Spies investigation provided clear evidence that illegal Pegasus surveillance continued under the current administration and that the targeted victims included journalists and a human rights defender investigating military abuses.","locator":"lines 71-72 and opening article summary","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Strong rights-organization synthesis of a forensic investigation, though it remains a secondary interpretation of technical findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to 2022 surveillance and press-freedom concerns, even though the infections themselves began earlier.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the current administration and the military apparatus under AMLO, but the report stops short of proving his personal operational order.","contrary_evidence":["The government denied the allegations and disputed the attribution.","The article is advocacy-oriented, so the operational conclusion should be triangulated with the technical report."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The right to protest is under threat in Mexico","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-05-18","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/05/right-to-protest-under-threat-mexico/","claim":"Amnesty said police violently and disproportionately repressed a peaceful student protest, used racist, classist and misogynistic insults, detained 93 people, and that the abuses remained unpunished a year later, illustrating a continuing threat to protest rights in Mexico.","locator":"article body and concluding paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed contemporaneous rights reporting with named incident, victims, and remedy failure, though the core incident occurred in 2021.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective 2021 incident published in 2022; useful for the 2022 pattern and aftermath, not a fresh 2022 crackdown order.","ruler_attribution":"The episode is attributed to state police under the national political environment; direct federal command is weaker than for the presidential press or electoral reforms.","contrary_evidence":["The incident predates the publication year, so it is better evidence of 2022 aftermath and pattern than of a new 2022 decision.","The article focuses on one state-level episode, not a full nationwide repression system."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.7"]}
Search and rejection summary:
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across gob.mx presidential transcripts, Reuters and Reuters-syndicated stories, Reuters Institute, ARTICLE 19, Amnesty, Citizen Lab/R3D, the U.S. State Department human-rights report, and AFP fact-checking.
- I kept 13 distinct URLs and collapsed repeated coverage of the same Reuters stories where the syndication was materially identical.
- I excluded 2023 and 2024 material that was out of period for this chapter, including later judicial-reform writeups and broader retrospective democracy essays that did not add 2022-specific ruler conduct.
- I did not re-fetch Freedom House, V-Dem, RSF, or other structured datasets because the local prior already supplied the relevant country-year context.
- Some gob.mx pages were 403-blocked on direct open, so I relied on the visible search snippets and on other accessible presidential transcripts where the same point was documented more cleanly.
- I rejected generic commentary pieces that repeated the same press-freedom critique without adding a new 2022 ruler-attributed fact, and I did not pad the dossier with duplicate coverage of the same press or electoral-reform episode.

Remaining gaps:
- The biggest gap is 4B.4: I found strong evidence of pressure on independent institutions, but much thinner direct 2022 evidence that AMLO strengthened courts, the INE, INAI, or other oversight bodies when they constrained him.
- I have good evidence on media hostility and election-rule manipulation attempts, but less on opposition victories outside the electoral-reform fight and less on whether he systematically tolerated criticism in contexts other than the press.
- The surveillance record is persuasive, but part of it is retrospective to 2019-2021; more search could still find direct 2022 procurement, oversight, or prosecution records to sharpen attribution.
- More work could also look for 2022 budget, appointment, or legal-compliance records on INE, INAI, and CNDH to separate inherited weakness from ruler-period choices more cleanly.

chapter_notes.5B:
**Reused evidence**
None. The prior nuclear, peace, and political-freedom items were not materially relevant to 5B, so I did not reuse them here.

**New evidence**
- IMF’s 2022 Article IV says Mexico’s recovery was gradual, inflation had become entrenched, and the government kept a broadly neutral fiscal stance while using largely untargeted subsidies to cushion the cost of living.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: 2022 Article IV Consultation-Press Release and Staff Report","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/11/04/mexico-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-and-staff-report-525448","claim":"Mexico’s recovery was gradual, inflation became entrenched, and the government maintained a broadly neutral fiscal stance in 2022 while using largely untargeted subsidies to mitigate the rise in the cost of living.","locator":"press release summary / opening summary paragraph","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF country surveillance with direct macro-fiscal assessment and a precise 2022 time fit.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period macro and fiscal conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the AMLO government because the fiscal stance and subsidy choices were government policy, but the inflation shock also reflected global conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF frames much of the pressure as a pandemic and global inflation shock, not purely a ruler-made problem.","The same source does not by itself prove distributional effectiveness or broad-based gains."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- OECD says Mexico’s macro framework preserved stability, but long-term growth remained low and productivity was held back by informality, financial exclusion, corruption, weak investment, and low female participation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: Mexico 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-02-01","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/2022/02/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_a2937d37.html","claim":"Mexico’s solid macroeconomic policy framework safeguarded stability, but long-run growth was low and productivity was hindered by informality, financial exclusion, corruption, weak investment, and low female labor-force participation.","locator":"executive summary / abstract","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative independent country survey from the OECD; broad but directly relevant structural diagnosis.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-year-adjacent baseline and inherited-constraint context for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly inherited and structural, though the policy environment under AMLO mattered for investment and competition.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD also says the macro policy framework remained solid, so the case is mixed rather than uniformly negative.","Low growth alone does not prove ruler culpability."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.5","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
- OECD’s detailed policy section says investment had been weak since 2015 and that uncertainty increased after electricity-market reform proposals, with the OECD urging certainty about contracts and regulatory stability.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Key Policy Insights: OECD Economic Surveys: Mexico 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-02-01","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/2022/02/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_a2937d37.html","claim":"Investment was weak and domestic policy uncertainty increased, especially after proposals to reform the electricity market; the OECD urged certainty about existing contracts and regulatory stability.","locator":"section on restarting investment and boosting productivity","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent policy analysis with explicit causation and clear relevance to ruler-period policy choices.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to 2022 policy settings and investor expectations.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to AMLO because the uncertainty arose from his government’s energy-policy proposals and regulatory direction.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD also says Mexico could benefit from U.S. recovery and nearshoring, so the environment was not uniformly adverse.","Investment weakness predates 2022, so this is partly inherited."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}
- OECD also says support to PEMEX had risen to about 1 percent of GDP and that PEMEX remained a significant sovereign risk.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Key Policy Insights: OECD Economic Surveys: Mexico 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-02-01","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/2022/02/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_a2937d37.html","claim":"Support to PEMEX had increased to about 1 percent of GDP and PEMEX remained a significant risk for the sovereign.","locator":"PEMEX and energy-policy recommendations section","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent expert assessment with a concrete fiscal magnitude and risk statement.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Baseline and policy-trajectory context for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to AMLO’s energy strategy and repeated support for state oil and power firms, though the fiscal risk is also structural.","contrary_evidence":["Energy security and state-capacity arguments can explain some support to PEMEX.","The OECD note is a policy warning, not proof that the spending was wholly wasteful."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- Reuters reported that the central bank raised rates to a record 9.25 percent because inflation was stubbornly high.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico, Colombia enact big rate hikes amid stubborn inflation","publisher":"Reuters / Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-09-29","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/bank-of-mexico-hikes-rate-to-record-high-of-925-as-expected-2902215","claim":"The Bank of Mexico raised its benchmark interest rate 75 basis points to a record 9.25 percent as inflation remained above target and persistent.","locator":"Reuters lines 419-428","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a concrete central-bank decision with clear numerical detail and date fit.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct 2022 macro context, though monetary policy was not under AMLO’s direct control.","ruler_attribution":"Only indirect attribution because Banxico is independent; this is mainly context for the economic environment AMLO governed in.","contrary_evidence":["This does not show an AMLO policy choice because the central bank is independent.","It mainly constrains credit and growth rather than proving any ruler failure."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9"]}
- Reuters also said the government’s inflation-response subsidies cost 575 billion pesos in 2022, mostly gasoline and energy subsidies, plus food-security support.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico anti-inflation subsidies come with $28 billion price tag","publisher":"Reuters / Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-08-02","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/mexico-antiinflation-subsidies-come-with-28-billion-price-tag-2860789","claim":"Mexico’s anti-inflation subsidies cost about 575 billion pesos in 2022, including 430 billion pesos for gasoline subsidies, 73 billion for domestic energy subsidies, and 69 billion for food-security measures.","locator":"Reuters story text / government figures cited in article","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters cites government figures and provides clear policy composition and fiscal scale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy response to inflation during the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the AMLO government because officials announced and defended the measures alongside the president.","contrary_evidence":["The measures were large and partly untargeted, so they may have been blunt rather than efficient.","The fiscal cost was high even if the policy eased consumer pain."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- The labor ministry said the 2022 minimum wage increase was 22 percent, the largest in 34 years, benefiting about 6 million workers.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Entra en vigor incremento al salario mínimo del 22%","publisher":"Secretaría del Trabajo y Previsión Social","publication_date":"2022-01-06","url":"https://www.gob.mx/stps/prensa/entra-en-vigor-incremento-al-salario-minimo-del-22?idiom=es","claim":"The 2022 minimum wage increase was 22 percent, the largest in 34 years, and it was said to benefit about 6 million workers directly.","locator":"press release opening bullets","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government labor announcement with a concrete policy change and target population.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy choice in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to AMLO’s administration because it was the government’s wage policy and was announced in the president’s orbit.","contrary_evidence":["Higher minimum wages can add inflation pressure, so the policy has tradeoffs.","The press release is promotional and does not by itself prove full distributional success."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.10"]}
- CONASAMI’s later impact study says the minimum wage had a negative elasticity with poverty and attributes most of the 2018-2022 poverty reduction to minimum-wage increases.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"El impacto del salario minimo en la pobreza","publisher":"Comisión Nacional de los Salarios Mínimos","publication_date":"2023-11-07","url":"https://www.gob.mx/conasami/es/articulos/el-impacto-del-salario-minimo-en-la-pobreza","claim":"The study estimates a negative elasticity of the minimum wage on poverty and says that 4.1 million of the 5.1 million people who left poverty between 2018 and 2022 can be attributed exclusively to minimum-wage increases.","locator":"article summary / study summary text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official analytical study with a concrete estimate, but it is retrospective and not fully independent of the policy agenda.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2018-2022, so it fits the target period as an after-the-fact estimate rather than contemporaneous evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to AMLO because the wage path was a core policy of his administration, but the estimate is retrospective and not solely his doing.","contrary_evidence":["The estimate is model-based and retrospective, not a contemporaneous census of causation.","The study does not isolate the minimum wage from other contributors such as remittances or the labor market."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.10"]}
- The finance ministry said nearshoring financing would include a 200 million dollar BID line and could unlock an estimated 35 billion dollars in potential gains, especially in the south-southeast.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Comunicado No. 47 Financiamiento para el impulso al nearshoring, con énfasis en la región del sur-sureste de México","publisher":"Secretaría de Hacienda y Crédito Público","publication_date":"2022-07-06","url":"https://www.gob.mx/shcp/prensa/comunicado-no-47-financiamiento-para-el-impulso-al-nearshoring-con-enfasis-en-la-region-del-sur-sureste-de-mexico","claim":"The finance ministry announced a 200 million dollar BID credit line to support nearshoring and said Mexico could gain 35 billion dollars, equivalent to 2.6 percent of GDP, especially in the south-southeast.","locator":"press release bullet points","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary policy announcement with concrete financing and projected gains, though the gain estimate is forward-looking.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy action and target-year industrial strategy.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to AMLO’s government because it was a federal financing plan announced by Hacienda under his administration.","contrary_evidence":["The projected gain is an estimate, not a realized outcome.","Nearshoring potential also depends on external demand and private investment, not only on AMLO."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
- The 2022 federal budget was presented as ensuring continuity for welfare programs, pensions, scholarships, producer support, and strategic infrastructure works.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PEF 2022 garantiza continuidad de programas sociales y obras de desarrollo","publisher":"Presidencia de la República 2018-2024 | Gobierno | gob.mx","publication_date":"2021-11-15","url":"https://www.gob.mx/amlo/prensa/pef-2022-garantiza-continuidad-de-programas-sociales-y-obras-de-desarrollo","claim":"The 2022 federal budget was presented as guaranteeing continuity for welfare programs, pensions, scholarships, producer support, and strategic infrastructure works such as Tren Maya, Dos Bocas, and the AIFA.","locator":"press release body / opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary budget-policy statement from the presidency with concrete spending priorities.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Budget for the target year, announced shortly before 2022 began.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to AMLO because this was the executive budget priority he promoted.","contrary_evidence":["Announcements do not prove implementation quality or economic payoff.","Strategic megaprojects may have large opportunity costs or weak returns."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
- In October 2022 AMLO publicly detailed direct school budgets, pensions, disability support, and a planned 25 percent pension increase.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"12.10.22 Versión estenográfica de la conferencia de prensa matutina del presidente Andrés Manuel López Obrador","publisher":"Presidencia de la República 2018-2024 | Gobierno | gob.mx","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/12-10-22-version-estenografica-de-la-conferencia-de-prensa-matutina-del-presidente-andres-manuel-lopez-obrador/","claim":"AMLO described direct school budgets, scholarships, pensions for older adults, disability support, and a planned 25 percent pension increase, all as part of his administration’s welfare system.","locator":"conference transcript / program recap section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ruler speech documenting policy priorities and implementation details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 ruler-period policy speech and program description.","ruler_attribution":"Fully attributable to AMLO as his own official morning conference and stated policy agenda.","contrary_evidence":["This is implementation rhetoric, not independent proof of final welfare outcomes.","The benefits may be offset by inflation and other macro pressures."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
- CONEVAL’s fourth-quarter 2022 labor-poverty report shows poverty labor fell, real labor income rose, and employment increased, but conditions were still worse than before the pandemic.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"EL CONEVAL PRESENTA INFORMACIÓN REFERENTE A LA POBREZA LABORAL AL CUARTO TRIMESTRE DE 2022","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2023-02-21","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/Medicion/Paginas/Pobreza_laboral_4toTrim2022.aspx","claim":"Between the fourth quarter of 2021 and the fourth quarter of 2022, labor poverty fell from 40.3 percent to 38.5 percent, real labor income per capita rose 4.3 percent, and the number of employed people increased by 1.7 million, though labor poverty remained above pre-pandemic levels.","locator":"bullet summary and discussion around lines 153-180","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary national statistics source with precise 2022 household-labor measures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the target year and closes the year with official 2022 labor-market evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to AMLO only in the sense that his wage and welfare policies were one input; the report itself also reflects external recovery and labor-market dynamics.","contrary_evidence":["The report does not isolate AMLO policy from the broader recovery.","Labor poverty was still above pre-pandemic levels."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
- CONEVAL’s 2022 multidimensional poverty release says poverty fell from 41.9 percent in 2018 to 36.3 percent in 2022, or from 51.9 million to 46.8 million people.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Comunicado No. 7, El CONEVAL presenta las estimaciones de pobreza multidimensional 2022","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2023-08-10","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/SalaPrensa/Comunicadosprensa/Documents/2023/Comunicado_07_Medicion_Pobreza_2022.pdf","claim":"Between 2018 and 2022, multidimensional poverty in Mexico fell from 41.9 percent to 36.3 percent, and the number of people in poverty fell from 51.9 million to 46.8 million.","locator":"page 0 and page 3 of the PDF, lines 13-19 and 136-147","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official poverty release with explicit national estimates and a clear target-year fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on the 2018-2022 period, directly covering the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to AMLO because it captures his full sexenio to 2022, but the report itself also reflects pandemic recovery and remittances.","contrary_evidence":["The period starts in 2018, so part of the change predates some 2022 policy choices.","The report does not itself isolate the contribution of one president versus external conditions."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}
- Reuters reported that remittances reached record levels in 2022 and that the rise was strongly helped by the U.S. labor market, which is a major external driver of Mexican household income.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remittances to Mexico again break record on back of strong U.S. labor market","publisher":"Reuters / Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-09-01","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/remittances-to-mexico-hit-new-record-high-in-july-2884463","claim":"Remittances to Mexico reached a record high in July 2022 at 5.3 billion dollars, and Reuters attributed part of the surge to the strong U.S. labor market.","locator":"Reuters lines 413-427","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting gives a concrete external condition that materially affected Mexican household income in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct 2022 household-income context.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly external rather than AMLO-attributable; useful as a counterweight against over-crediting domestic policy for household gains.","contrary_evidence":["The growth came largely from conditions in the United States, not Mexican policy.","High inflation meant remittances did not translate one-for-one into better real purchasing power."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10"]}
- Reuters said AMLO’s electricity overhaul was defeated in Congress after critics argued it would hurt investment and breach obligations.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexican president's contentious electricity overhaul defeated in Congress","publisher":"Reuters / Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-04-17","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/commodities-news/mexican-lawmakers-to-vote-on-presidents-contentious-electricity-overhaul-defeated-in-congress-2804968","claim":"AMLO’s plan to increase state control of electricity generation was defeated in Congress, with opponents warning it would hurt investment and breach international obligations.","locator":"Reuters article text / lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a concrete legislative defeat with direct policy implications for investment and competition.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy conflict in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable because it was AMLO’s plan and his governing coalition pushed it.","contrary_evidence":["AMLO framed the overhaul as protecting public interest and energy sovereignty.","Congress defeated the constitutional version, so the final policy outcome was partly blocked rather than fully enacted."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.6","5B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across IMF, OECD, CONEVAL, INEGI, Banxico, STPS, SHCP, AMLO/gob.mx, Reuters, Reuters-syndicated coverage, and a few local outlets. I kept 12 distinct URLs and merged closely related claims where they came from the same document and same policy episode.

I rejected or downweighted:
- Out-of-period material from 2023 to 2025 that did not add 2022-specific ruler evidence, except when used as retrospective analysis of the 2018-2022 period.
- Duplicate Reuters syndications of the same underlying story.
- Purely descriptive pages that restated baseline macro context without adding ruler-attributed policy detail.
- Several gob.mx pages that were blocked on direct open, although their search snippets were still usable when they contained concrete 2022 policy details.
- Generic commentary on remittances, inflation, or nearshoring that did not add a distinct claim beyond a stronger document already retained.

**Remaining gaps**
- The weakest area is 5B.2. I have good evidence on AMLO’s policy direction, but less direct evidence about whether he appointed and empowered genuinely independent economic professionals versus loyalists in the full 2022 team.
- I have strong evidence on minimum wage, welfare transfers, inflation response, and some investment policy, but less on firm-level competition, entrepreneurship, property-rights predictability, and regulatory quality beyond the electricity and PEMEX cases.
- The poverty and labor-income improvements are real, but causation is mixed. More search would help separate AMLO’s wage and transfer policy from U.S. labor demand, remittances, oil prices, and pandemic rebound.
- I still do not have a fully independent assessment of whether the 2022 growth trajectory was durable or mostly cyclical. Additional work on investment, productivity, and household consumption would tighten that judgment.

chapter_notes.6B:
