Search and rejection summary
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across official Mexican welfare releases, CONEVAL evaluations, UNICEF reports, OECD surveys, World Bank poverty pages, and labor-policy releases.
- I kept 19 source-claim units from 16 distinct URLs and collapsed overlapping government program notices where a later page repeated the same beneficiary counts without adding a materially new claim.
- I rejected or downgraded generic budget pages, index/catalog pages, and 2025-2026 program pages unless they explicitly reported 2022 conditions or a 2018-2022 retrospective estimate.
- I treated broad macroeconomic pages as context only when they added a welfare connection and did not simply restate already-covered baseline priors.
- I did not re-fetch the local WHO/UNDP/WDI priors you provided; I used them as background and only added narrative evidence where ruler attribution or implementation details were missing.

Remaining gaps
- I still lack strong independent 2022 evidence on service quality, not just coverage or funding, for health and education.
- Distributional evidence is still thin for exact state-by-state, indigenous, disability, and gender effects of specific federal programs in 2022.
- The causal split between AMLO policy, post-pandemic rebound, U.S. labor demand, remittances, and structural trends remains only partly resolved.
- I also lack a strong independent audit of whether direct transfers fully avoided local patronage or political conditionality everywhere.

No score assigned.

